"The surah begins with our Prophet (peace be upon him) frowning and turning his back. It is understood that the pronouns in the verbs 'he frowned' and 'turned away' here refer to the Messenger of Allah; however, with the transition from the third person to the second person (the art of iltifāt / shift of perspective), attention is drawn to the fact that warning and admonition must first begin with the warner himself. The reason is the arrival of a blind Companion (Abdullah ibn Umm Maktum) to him. The attribute 'blind' emphasizes his excuse and his need for guidance; it is pointed out that frowning and turning away was not appropriate.\n\nIn the verses, it is expressed that the blind one might perhaps be purified from sins or take heed and benefit. The Prophet's turning toward the wealthy who deem themselves self-sufficient rather than toward this needy person is criticized. The expression 'you attend to him' means giving excessive importance to the guidance of those wealthy individuals. Yet, he is not responsible if the self-sufficient one does not purify himself; the true responsibility is to turn toward those who are eager, like the blind man who comes striving and fearing Allah. The words 'you pay no heed to him' reinforce the impropriety of this neglect.\n\nNext comes the warning, 'No, indeed, do not do so!' For the Quran is an admonition, a reminder. Whoever wills may ponder it and take heed. Here, the expression 'whoever wills' does not mean leaving the person free, but rather conveys a warning and a threat. The Quran is in noble, exalted, and purified sheets, in the hands of scribes (angels or writers). These scribes are noble and righteous; they are called 'safara'. The dignity and purity of the sheets demonstrate the majesty of the Quran and that it is worthy of reverence.\n\nHuman ingratitude is condemned: 'Cursed is man; how ungrateful he is!' Man must not forget that Allah created him from a drop of fluid and proportioned him in the finest form, then eased his way, caused him to die and be buried, and will resurrect him whenever He wills. In spite of this, man has not fully carried out Allah's command. This deficiency is a general characteristic of humankind; grateful servants are few.\n\nLet man look at his food: Allah pours down water abundantly, splits the earth open, and causes crops, grapes, clover, olive groves, date palms, dense gardens, fruits, and pastures to grow there. These are beneficial for humans and animals. These blessings serve as a reminder of Allah's power and His favors that require gratitude.\n\nWhen that deafening noise (the Day of Resurrection) arrives, a person will flee from his brother, his mother, his father, his spouse, and his sons. For on that Day, every person will have a concern sufficient to preoccupy him. On that Day, there are faces that will be shining, laughing, and rejoicing; and there are faces that will be dust-stained and covered in darkness. It is these latter faces that belong to the disbelievers and the transgressors.\n\nAllah causes various blessings to grow on the earth for humans and animals: grains, grapes, fresh clover, vegetables, olives, date palms, and dense, tangled gardens. It is Allah the Almighty who grows and matures all of these. The word \"abb\" mentioned in these verses means meadow, pasture, vegetables, or animal fodder; it inherently carries the meaning of preparation and being prepared.\n\nAccording to what is narrated from Abu Bakr and Umar, when they were asked about what the word \"abb\" was, they preferred, without going into excessive detail on this matter, to act upon what the Quran has clearly stated and to entrust what is unknown to Allah. Umar emphasized that delving into the details of such words is burdensome and an undue strain, and that what is truly important is to give thanks for the blessings.\n\nThree important points can be drawn from these narrations: First, there are concise/general (mujmal) words in the Quran whose meanings not everyone can comprehend at all times, and one must believe in them in brief. Second, the word \"abb\", like \"mar'ā\" (pasture) in the previous surah, is a comprehensive expression encompassing many plants that humans and animals benefit from. Third, the meaning of 'preparation' inherent in this word points to the necessary preparations for the life of the Hereafter.\n\nWhen the Day of Resurrection arrives, a deafening, violent sound called \"al-Sakhkhah\" will burst forth. This sound is defined by two characteristics: first, making itself heard and perceived above all else; second, deafening the ears due to its intensity in such a way that no other sound can be heard. This is an unparalleled example of eloquence expressing the terror of the Resurrection.\n\nOn that Day, everyone falls into their own concern; a person flees from his brother, his parents, his spouse, and his children. For everyone has a matter sufficient to keep them occupied. As stated in the hadith, people are gathered naked, barefoot, and bathed in sweat; each person looks only to their own state.\n\nOn the Day of Resurrection, some faces are bright, smiling, and joyous. These are the faces of those who have been purified through reverence for Allah, faith, and righteous deeds. In some commentaries, it is stated that this radiance is the result of acts of worship such as the night prayer, ablution, or jihad. Other faces, however, are covered in dust, dark, and miserable; these are the disbelievers and the wicked. Before that Day arrives, man should believe, refrain from sin, and strive to attain salvation with an untarnished face."